628	}# Workflow structure and routing recipe
629	
630	A shape that works for any collections-style brief. Fill every rule, value, field name, enum, outcome and reason
631	from THE BRIEF and THE TOOL API FILE you were given. Node names below are suggestions; stage contents come from the brief.
632	
633	## Principle
634	
635	The agent model is small. It follows one short task well and many mixed tasks badly. So:
636	- one conversation node per stage of the call, each with only the tools of that stage;
637	- the call moves between stages on **tool results** (deterministic `equals` / `greater_than` on result variables),
638	  never on what the customer said (`matches_intent`) when a tool result can decide it;
639	- every node that can close the call has the close tool, and a transition on the close tool's success sends the
640	  call to an `end_call` node. Nothing else hangs up.
641	
642	## Stages (adapt to the brief)
643	
644	```text
645	opening (conversation)          who is on the line; third-party branch (callback / wrong number / deceased / unclear)
646	   │  tools: the callback tool, the close tool
647	   │  → identity            when the extracted variable `party` equals "customer"  (extraction_schema enum, default "unknown")
648	   │  → hang_up             when close tool succeeded
649	identity (conversation)         security questions, one verify call per answer
650	   │  tools: verify tool, close tool, transfer tool (if the brief allows a human request before verification)
651	   │  post-hook on verify adds data.what_to_do_next (next question / ask again / hint retry / failed → close)
652	   │  → load_account        when verify response says all verified (test "true" AND "True")
653	   │  → hang_up             when close tool succeeded
654	load_account (api_request, passthrough, silent)   pre_action = account lookup, node-level response_mapping data.<list>
655	   │  → prepare (always)
656	prepare (code, passthrough, silent)               Python: every number/date the conversation needs, as STRINGS:
657	   │  per-product summary line, floors, bands, deadlines, offer dates, "today", the business-day list for the next
658	   │  weeks, which product is current. Read the account list defensively (list / JSON string / flattened keys).
659	   │  → negotiate (always)
660	negotiate (conversation)        state the debt, all the promise logic, objections, transfers, notes, closing
661	   │  tools: promise tool, transfer tool, note tool (only if the brief uses it), close tool
662	   │  task_message shows the computed facts with {{vars}}, never asks the model to do calendar math
663	   │  post-hook on the promise tool adds what_to_do_next ("say it is recorded and the confirmation sentence" /
664	   │  "it failed: try once more" / "failed twice: say it could not be recorded, then close with <reason>")
665	   │  → hang_up             when close tool succeeded
666	hang_up (end_call)              message_mode "none", on_enter silent. The goodbye is said by the previous node.
667	```
668	
669	Multi-product briefs: keep ONE negotiate node and let the code node build an ordered product list with a summary
670	per product; the prompt walks through them in order (one promise per product). Do not create one node per product.
671	In the negotiate prompt, spell out the per-item bookkeeping the brief asks for (only what the brief says):
672	- each item's own deadline, floor/minimum and attempt count come from that item's fields (`deadline`,
673	  `floor_amount`, `persuasion_attempts` if the hook added them); a date valid for one item can be past another's deadline;
674	- keep a running list in the prompt's words: item id → agreed amount + date / refused + reason / not yet discussed;
675	- read back all agreed items together once (each with its own amount and date), get one confirmation, then call the
676	  write tool exactly ONCE per agreed item with that item's id; after a SUCCESS never call it again for that item;
677	- items without a write get the brief's per-item note (if the brief has one), once per item, BEFORE the close tool;
678	  never call any tool after the close tool.
679	
680	## Exact routing patterns
681	
682	Close tool → end node (put this FIRST in the transitions of every conversation node that has the close tool):
683	```json
684	{ "target_node": "hang_up", "description": "The call was closed",
685	  "condition": { "logic": "or", "conditions": [
686	    { "variable": "close_call_success_count", "operator": "greater_than", "operand": 0 } ] } }
687	```
688	Only use it if the close tool's post-hook makes failures count as failures; safer: test the response too:
689	```json
690	{ "logic": "or", "conditions": [
691	  { "variable": "close_call_response.data.ok", "operator": "equals", "operand": "true" },
692	  { "variable": "close_call_response.data.ok", "operator": "equals", "operand": "True" } ] }
693	```
694	
695	Identity passed (boolean from the API, test both spellings):
696	```json
697	{ "target_node": "load_account", "description": "Identity verified",
698	  "condition": { "logic": "or", "conditions": [
699	    { "variable": "verify_identity_response.data.all_verified", "operator": "equals", "operand": "true" },
700	    { "variable": "verify_identity_response.data.all_verified", "operator": "equals", "operand": "True" } ] } }
701	```
702	(Use the field names of the tool API file you were given.)
703	
704	Who is on the line (opening): an `extraction_schema` variable `party` with enum such as
705	`customer | other_person | unknown`, default `unknown`, and transitions with `equals`. Only route to identity on a
706	clear confirmation as the brief defines it; let the opening node handle everything else itself.
707	
708	## Tool rules in every node
709	
710	- Each `api_call` tool: `post_process_code` that pops `header`, and adds `data.what_to_do_next`: one plain sentence
711	  saying what the result means and what to say or call next, derived from the brief's rule for that tool
712	  (success, `ok: false`, specific hints/errors). Wrap in `try/except Exception: pass`.
713	- A pre-hook must never point `config['url']` at localhost / 127.0.0.1 / any other host: the platform refuses
714	  it ("Localhost URLs are not allowed") and the turn breaks. To stop a call that would break a brief rule (date
715	  outside the window, amount below the floor, a second close), the pre-hook removes a required body field (so the
716	  bank answers `ok: false` and records nothing) and stores the reason in a `vars` flag; the post-hook then puts
717	  that reason into `data.what_to_do_next` ("not recorded because ...; offer ... instead").
718	- Close tool: `outcome` and `reason` are model-filled leaves with the enums of the API file; descriptions list
719	  the brief's outcome table (which reason belongs to which outcome). Pre-hook drops `reason` for outcomes that the
720	  brief says take none.
721	- `customer_id` and other ids that the model must not invent: `default: "{{customer_id}}"`; product ids: list them
722	  in the code node's summary so the model copies them exactly.
723	- The task_message of each node starts with: "After every tool result, speak to the customer or call the next
724	  tool. Never end a turn with nothing. Call one tool per message. Before calling the close tool say the goodbye."
725	- Set `max_node_turns` high (40-60) on conversation nodes; a whole negotiation can take many turns.
726	
727	## Prompt format: numbered rules + SAY NEXT (every conversation node)
728	
729	The agent model is small and runs each case several times; a case must pass every run. Long prose prompts give a
730	different reading each run. Write every conversation node's `task_message` in this fixed shape, nothing else:
731	
732	```text
733	STAGE: <NAME>. <one line: the goal of this stage>
734	FACTS: <the {{vars}} the stage needs; {{date_facts}} where dates matter>
735	RULES:
736	1. <one rule, one sentence, at most ~25 words; an exact sentence the brief requires goes in quotes>
737	2. ...
738	SAY NEXT:
739	- AFTER <tool> SUCCESS: say "<the exact words, with {{vars}}>", then <next action: call <tool> / ask ... / close with <outcome>/<reason>>.
740	- AFTER <tool> FAILED: say "<the exact words>", then <next action per the brief's failure rule>.
741	- AFTER <tool> <other result the brief names, e.g. not verified / refused / already recorded>: say "...", then ....
742	```
743	
744	- One numbered rule per brief rule that applies in this stage; never merge two rules into one line, never use
745	  "etc." or "as usual". Order the rules in the order the call meets them (first thing to say first).
746	- SAY NEXT has a SUCCESS line AND a FAILED line for EVERY tool of the node (plus a line per other result the brief
747	  names). Each line says the words to speak and the single next action, so the model never has to invent what
748	  comes after a tool result. The close tool's lines: SUCCESS: say nothing more; FAILED: say goodbye once, then stop.
749	- The same words go into that tool's post-hook `what_to_do_next`, so prompt and tool result never disagree.
750	- `builder/lint_workflow.py` reports an ERROR for a conversation node with tools whose task_message has no
751	  numbered RULES or no SAY NEXT SUCCESS/FAILED line for one of its tools.
752	
753	## Global nodes
754	
755	Only for interrupts that are identical everywhere AND do not need tools (e.g. "are you a robot?" → fixed line,
756	passthrough back to `__previous__`). Exclude `hang_up`. Most interrupts (human request, dispute, hardship) need
757	tools and outcome logic: handle them inside the stage node instead.
758	
759	## Before saving
760	
761	Run `python3 builder/lint_workflow.py out/<slot>.workflow.json` and fix every ERROR it prints.
762

